  "subjective": "CHIEF COMPLAINT: Sore on right foot that is not healing\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS: The patient reports a sore on the right foot that has been present for about two to three weeks. The patient had a blister that worsened and now has a non-healing wound. The patient saw their primary care physician (PCP) who referred them to the doctor. The patient had a course of antibiotics but the wound did not improve. The patient has neuropathy and used new hiking shoes that caused a blister. The patient has had neuropathy and has a history of seeing a podiatrist but the podiatrist moved and the patient was unable to find another one. The patient has had a history of neuropathy and has a history of wearing Hoka shoes, which they love. The patient's last hemoglobin A1c (HbA1c) was 8.1%.\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS: The patient denies fever, chills, or drainage from the wound. The patient reports that the wound sometimes sticks to the sock when taking it off. The patient has a history of neuropathy and has a history of wearing Hoka shoes, which they love. The patient's diabetes is under control with a recent HbA1c of 8.1%.",
  "objective_exam": "On physical examination, the patient's vital signs were: temperature 98.1°C, heart rate 72 bpm, respiratory rate 16 breaths/min, blood pressure 105/65 mmHg. The patient's foot examination revealed a 1x2 inch circular wound on the dorsal aspect of the lateral right foot, proximal to the right fifth metatarsophalangeal (MTP) joint. There was some yellow slough with minimal granulation tissue. There was no surrounding erythema or cellulitis, no evidence of fluid collection, no necrosis, and no odor. There was no bony exposure on vascular examination, with palpable bilateral femoral and popliteal pulses and Doppler signs present. The patient did not report any pain when the wound was touched.",
  "objective_results": "The patient's right foot X-ray showed no evidence of osteomyelitis, indicating no evidence of bone infection. The patient's ankle brachial index (ABI) was not performed.",
  "assessment_and_plan": "Assessment: Diabetic foot ulcer. Plan: Order an ankle brachial index (ABI) to determine the blood supply to the foot. Perform debridement in the office to remove dead tissue. Prescribe a collagenase ointment to be applied to the wound once daily and covered with a dry sterile dressing. Continue until the wound shrinks and a nice pink tissue is present. Recommend wearing a surgical shoe to take pressure off the area. Plan to see the patient in two weeks to assess wound healing and consider referral to a vascular specialist if the ABI indicates suboptimal blood supply for wound healing. Address the patient's diabetes by getting it under control and possibly referring for a diabetic consult to modify diet and improve HbA1c."
}

