  "objective_exam": "PHYSICAL EXAMINATION: Right hand: Pain on flexion and extension of the wrist. Positive Tinel's sign when tapping on the inside of the wrist. Grip strength is slightly weaker on the right hand compared to the left.",
  "objective_results": "EMG/nerve conduction study ordered.",
  "assessment_and_plan": "ASSessment AND PLAN: Carpal tunnel syndrome is suspected. Plan includes wearing a wrist splint at night. Continue ibuprofen at 600 mg four times daily. EMG/nerve conduction study will guide further management. If positive, consider physical therapy or surgery. Patient prefers conservative treatment initially."
}

{
  "subjective": "CHIEF COMPLAINT: Right elbow pain following a lacrosse game with children.\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS: The patient reports a sudden onset of sharp pain in the right elbow, which radiates down the forearm, following a game with his 13-year-old son. He has taken ibuprofen but the pain persists.\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS: No chest pain, no abdominal pain.",
