{
  "subjective": "CHIEF COMPLAINT: Fatigue, dizziness, and decreased energy over the past few months.\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS: Joyce, a 50-year-old female with a past medical history of COPD and congestive heart failure, presents with an abnormal lab finding of low hemoglobin. She reports feeling very tired and exhausted, with a decline in her exercise routine. She denies blood in stools, dark or black-colored stools, or spotting with her periods. She does not report chest pain, shortness of breath, fever, chills, or body aches. She has noticed a slight weight loss, but it is attributed to her exercise routine. She has not experienced swelling in her legs or any other symptoms related to her congestive heart failure. She has a good support system with her boyfriend and brother.\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS: No significant symptoms related to COPD or congestive heart failure were reported.",
  "objective_exam": "On physical examination, Joyce's vital signs were normal. Her heart sounds were good, but a slight systolic heart murmur was heard (2/6). Her lungs were clear, and her abdomen was tender to palpation in the right lower quadrant. No lower extremity edema was noted.",
  "objective_results": "Hemoglobin level: 8.2 g/dL (normal range: 13-18 g/dL). Anemia labs are pending.",
  "assessment_and_plan": "Assessment: Anemia, COPD, and congestive heart failure.\nPlan: Schedule endoscopy and colonoscopy to rule out gastrointestinal bleeding. Continue to monitor hemoglobin levels. Continue to follow up with the gastroenterology clinic. Continue to take toprol and lisinopril for COPD. Continue diuretic dosing of 20 mg of furosemide daily for congestive heart failure. Order referral to gastroenterology for endoscopy and colonoscopy."
}

{
  "subjective": "CHIEF COMPLAINT: Recurrent chest pain\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS: Evelyn, a 56-year-old female with a past medical history of coronary artery disease, prior carpal tunnel release, and a herniated disc, presents for her annual exam. She reports that her herniated disc has been doing well without back pain or movement limitations. She has been back to work and has full range of motion in her hands without numbness or tingling. Over the last month, she has experienced chest pain that occurs when walking and resolves with rest. The pain feels like a burn and occurs about three times a week. She denies chest pain at rest. She has had nasal congestion due to allergies but no lower extremity edema, muscle aches, or fever/chills. She has a slight three out of six systolic ejection murmur and no carotid bruits. She has healed surgical scars on her right wrist.\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS: No new symptoms noted except for recurrent chest pain and nasal congestion.",
  "objective_exam": "The physical examination revealed a slight three out of six systolic ejection murmur, clear lungs, no carotid bruits, and no lower extremity edema. Healed surgical scars were noted on the right wrist.",
  "objective_results": "Echocardiogram: Slightly low pumping function of the heart at 45%, slight mitral regurgitation (murmur heard). ECG: Stable, reflecting prior coronary artery disease with no new changes. Stress test ordered. Lipid panel ordered. Starting Crestor 20 mg, once a day.",
  "assessment_and_plan": "From the carpal tunnel release, no further intervention is needed. For the herniated disc, no further steroid injection or physical therapy is required. The most concerning issue is the recurrent chest pain. Continue Aspirin and Toprol. Order a stress test to evaluate for more blockages causing the chest discomfort. Start Crestor 20 mg, once a day. The nurse will schedule the stress test and other appointments."
}

