{
  "subjective": "CHIEF COMPLAINT: Followup of chronic problems - depression, diabetes, and hypertension.\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS: Bruce reports a busy year with increased anxiety and stress, particularly around the holidays. He mentions a vacation planned for the new year. He has been experiencing symptoms of depression but has not pursued medication. He has been taking long walks in the summer but has not been able to continue due to the changing seasons. He is planning to monitor his sugar intake during the holidays but has not had any significant issues. He is still taking metformin and lisinopril. He has not had any chest pain, shortness of breath, or weight loss. He has not had any blood in his stool or edema.\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS: No significant symptoms reported.",
  "objective_exam": "PHYSICAL EXAMINATION: Bruce's blood pressure was within normal limits. A slight two out of six systolic ejection murmur was heard at the left base. His lungs were clear. Bilateral lower extremities had one plus non-pitting edema. EKG and diabetes logs were reviewed.",
  "objective_results": "RESULTS: EKG: Normal. Hemoglobin A1c: Elevated at 8%. No carotid bruits. No chest pain, shortness of breath, or weight loss. No blood in stool. No edema. Ejection murmur at the left base. Bilateral lower extremities have one plus non-pitting edema. No significant findings on diabetes logs.",
  "assessment_and_plan": "ASSESSMENT AND PLAN: Depression: Refer to psychiatry for talk therapy. Diabetes: Order a hemoglobin A1c in the next couple of months and increase metformin to 1000 mg twice a day. Order a lipid panel. Hypertension: Order an echocardiogram to recheck the heart murmur. Continue lisinopril at 20 mg daily. No further questions."
}

{
  "subjective": "CHIEF COMPLAINT: Numbness and tingling in both hands, with worsening over the last month. Tingling and numbness are more pronounced on the right side, extending from the middle and ring finger up to the forearm. Mild stiffness in the neck, with a contusion from the car accident. Soreness, sometimes with throbbing, especially when using the hands. Mild loss of sensation in the hands or fingers, but still able to feel. History of hypertension and anxiety, with controlled prediabetes. No history of thyroid issues.",
