{
  "subjective": "CHIEF COMPLAINT: Bilateral hand numbness and tingling for a few weeks.\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS: Patient reports numbness and tingling in both hands, more noticeable since a car accident in December. The tingling and numbness are more pronounced on the right side, from the middle and ring finger up to the forearm, and on the left side, from the pinky and ring finger. The tingling is sometimes accompanied by a throbbing sensation, but the pain resolves when the tingling occurs. The numbness and tingling are more noticeable at night and throughout the day. The patient experiences mild stiffness in her neck, which she attributes to a contusion from the accident. She has been taking nerve pain medication but has not been consistent with it. She has a history of chronic kidney disease, gout, hypertension, anxiety, GERD, and diabetes.\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS: Constitutional: fatigue, weight gain. GI: excessive appetite, bloating. Eyes: floaters. Cardiovascular: shortness of breath, leg swelling. Endocrine: positive diabetes, always hungry. Skin: nail bed trenches, dryness, itching. Musculoskeletal: gout, muscle pain. Neurological: parasthesia, bilateral hands.",
  "objective_exam": "PHYSICAL EXAMINATION: Neurological: Sensations normal except for diminished pin prick sensation in the third and fourth fingers on the right, and the fourth and fifth fingers on the left. Reflexes are one plus and symmetric, two plus at the knees and toes, which are downgoing bilaterally. Tinel's sign is positive at both wrists and both elbows.",
  "objective_results": "RESULTS: Bilateral carpal tunnel syndrome, question of bilateral cubital tunnel syndrome.",
  "assessment_and_plan": "ASSESSMENT AND PLAN: Bilateral carpal tunnel syndrome, question of bilateral cubital tunnel syndrome. Scheduled for EMG, NCV. If symptoms do not resolve, referral to hand clinic for possible symptomatic injections or carpal tunnel release surgery. Encouraged to wear splints. End of note."
}

{
  "subjective": "CHIEF COMPLAINT: Acid reflux\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS: The patient reports having acid reflux for several years, which has worsened over the past few years. She takes 40 milligrams of omeprazole twice daily, but her symptoms are not well-controlled. She experiences burning sensations in her chest and throat, especially after eating spicy foods, carbonated beverages, citrus, and tomato-based products. She has not had any difficulty swallowing, but she has lost her appetite for the past two months due to work stress. She has a family history of esophageal cancer on her mother's side and has had acid reflux and taken medications for it in the past. She has not had an upper endoscopy in the last 10 years.\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS: The patient denies any abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, changes in bowel movements, constipation, diarrhea, or rectal bleeding. She has well-healed abdominal surgical scars from a surgery when she was a baby, and she has a history of anxiety and seasonal allergies.",
  "objective_exam": "PHYSICAL EXAMINATION: Normal physical exam except for well-healed abdominal surgical scars noted.",
  "objective_results": "RESULTS: No abnormal findings on physical exam.",
  "assessment_and_plan": "ASSESSMENT AND PLAN: The patient has a history of gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) with poorly controlled symptoms despite taking omeprazole. She has identified trigger foods and high stress as triggers. The plan includes dietary modifications, stress management, and a trial of a different proton pump inhibitor (PPI) such as Nexium. She will be scheduled for an upper endoscopy (EGD) to look for potential causes of GERD. If the EGD is normal and symptoms persist despite a trial of Nexium, a trial of Protonix and a ph-impedance study will be considered to differentiate between functional etiology and hypersensitivity. The patient will be followed up in the office for the EGD and to discuss the results and progress with the dietary, stress, and medication changes."
}

{
  "subjective": "CHIEF COMPLAINT: Acute to chronic constipation\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS: The patient reports experiencing increasing concerns for spasms, lightheadedness, significant constipation, and diaphoresis. She was admitted to the hospital due to febrile symptoms, elevated white blood cell count, and concerns for a urinary tract infection. She was treated with intravenous antibiotic therapy and eventually discharged after clinical improvement. She feels better now and has a supra-pubic catheter. She believes her hospitalization was more likely due to her dysreflexia from her quadriplegia, which typically occurs. She feels her hospitalization was less likely a true urinary tract infection and was related to her constipation. She had a good bowel regimen, including taking miralax regularly and lactulose for severe constipation. She had a good discussion regarding her leukocytosis.\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS: The patient reports no pain or discomfort. She has a history of quadriplegia due to a prior spinal cord injury. She is hesitant about the COVID-19 vaccine and believes it is too soon to know the side effects. She takes vitamin C, multivitamins, and is closely monitored for respiratory issues. She has not had a flu shot in the past year.\n",
  "objective_exam": "The patient is not experiencing pain or discomfort. She has a history of quadriplegia due to a prior spinal cord injury. She is not taking any medications at the time of the visit.",
  "objective_results": "The patient's white blood cell count was elevated during her hospitalization, but the cultures were not very impressive. She has a supra-pubic catheter and has not had a regular bowel movement in a while, even though she takes miralax daily. She has not had a flu shot in the past year.",
  "assessment_and_plan": "The patient is being referred for updated fasting labs in the next three to four weeks to repeat the CBC and check her white blood cells. The patient is being advised to get vaccinated against COVID-19, as it is the only true preventative mechanism. She is being encouraged to make the decision to get vaccinated based on the risks and benefits. She is being advised to wait a couple of weeks before getting blood work done for cholesterol and to come back in two to three weeks for a follow-up visit."
}

